Patch 83 (dmsynth p37) -- drop a recovered suspend gap from the synth timeline.

Patch 60 restores the DirectSound transport after it stops or stalls, but the
transport and DirectMusic sample timeline are independent.  A live p35 process
after repeated deep suspend/resume had music but delayed steps and attacks.  A
bounded external read of its synth_sink proved the mapping itself was stale:

  activate_time + written_samples_time - master_time = -18.302 seconds

That negative value means a gameplay note stamped with the current reference
time maps roughly 18 seconds ahead of the sample position the render thread is
currently producing.  The user's approximately ten-second report was taken
while this gap was already being rendered away.  PipeWire remained running at
10 ms node latency with a 30 ms forced quantum, and music was unaffected, so an
endpoint-wide delay does not explain the observation.

When patch 60 actually re-arms the DirectSound transport, mark the timeline for
review.  The render thread owns sink->written and consumes that marker.  If its
mapping is more than 250 ms behind the live DirectSound write lead, move
activate_time forward by exactly that deficit and move all already queued MIDI
events backward by the equivalent sample count.  New RefTimeToSample mappings
and queue insertion are serialized with the same synth lock so an event cannot
cross the rebase half-converted.  Ordinary recovery with no material clock gap
is a no-op.

No new thread, import, wall clock, polling loop or hot-thread logging is added.
The p37 PE has the same DLL import set as p35.  The function-level binary diff
is limited to PlayBuffer, render/recovery call paths, three queue-lock helpers,
and the compiler's unsigned 64-bit division helper used by the bounded timing
arithmetic.

Candidate binary:

  binaries/dmsynth_p37_resume_timeline.dll
  b11c9b6ba2f1d27fcdea822fff37f62187862aa7aeb5527d2efd2a159778ede8

This patch is relative to dmsynth p35 (patch 60 plus the inherited recorded
dmsynth chain), not pristine Wine 11.0.  Status at export: candidate, not yet
the FINALPLAY19 default.

